<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=jacquard-machine">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacquard machine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jacquard loom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jacquard machine is a device fitted to a loom that automates the weaving of complex patterns such as brocade and damask, controlled by a chain of punched cards; it was patented by Joseph Marie Jacquard in 1804. It built on earlier inventions by Basile Bouchon, Jean Baptiste Falcon and Jacques Vaucanson, and made possible the automatic production of unlimited varieties of complex patterns. Its use of replaceable punched cards to control a sequence of operations inspired Charles Babbage's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analytical-engine">Analytical Engine</a> and is considered an important step in the history of computing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacquard-machine.jpg" alt="Jacquard machine" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjoertvedt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sj%C3%B6llingstad_IMG_3286_Jacquard_german_1939_Sachsische_Webstuhlfabrik_(Louis_Sch%C3%B6nherr)_Chemnitz.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1804</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Joseph Marie Jacquard</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The article says the machine was based on earlier inventions by Basile Bouchon (1725), Jean Baptiste Falcon (1728) and Jacques Vaucanson (1740), that Jacquard 'possibly combined mechanical elements of other inventors, but certainly innovated', and that a first prototype of a Jacquard-type loom was made in the second half of the 15th century by an Italian weaver known as Jean le Calabrais.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sericulture">Silk<span>c. 3500 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/analytical-engine">Analytical Engine<span>1837</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=jacquard-machine">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
